{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Do not turn to their offering</b> — According to its plain sense, this refers to the incense that they will offer before You tomorrow: do not turn to it. And its midrashic interpretation: he said, I know that they have a share in the public daily offerings — let their share not find favor before You. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 16:15). And it is not correct in my eyes that this should refer to the incense, because concerning Dathan and Abiram he says the same thing, since he was angered by their words, and they were not among the assembly gathered to offer incense. But its plain sense is that since these men desired the priesthood in order to offer sacrifice, Moses said: \"Do not turn to their offering\" — do not turn to whatever sacrifice they may offer before You, nor to any prayer they may pray to You, for all sacrifices and also prayer are called מנחה in Scripture. And Onkelos too rendered (Onkelos on Numbers 16:15) קורבנהון — \"their offering,\" meaning any thing that they may offer before You." "And the sense of <b>not one donkey of theirs have I taken</b> — he said: whatever authority I exercise over them, I have never taken from them even a single donkey for my own use, in the manner of kings or princes, for this is the prerogative of royalty, as it is written: \"He will take your donkeys and put them to his work\" (1 Samuel 8:16). And this is the sense of Onkelos's rendering (Onkelos on Numbers 16:15) שחרית, for thus the king's compulsory labor is called שחרא (Bava Batra 47). He mentioned the smallest thing among the laws of kingship, and then went back and said, \"nor have I wronged a single one of them\" — to set him to my chariot and to my work as would befit a king, or to pervert his justice and dishonor his dignity, for he subsumed all wrongs under this term."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The midrashic interpretation cited from Rashi refers to the word מנחתם as related to the public offerings (תמידי צבור); the translation preserves this reading faithfully.", 1 "The Aramaic terms שחרית (Onkelos) and שחרא (in the Talmudic passage) both refer to the king's right of angaria (compulsory service); the Hebrew text transliterates them and Ramban explains their connection to the donkey mentioned in 1 Samuel."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:15.787560176Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}